@attribute [Route($"{GlobalValues.Root_Path_Components}/{GlobalValues.Component_Path_Part_GoTo}/{GlobalValues.Common_Page_Path_Overview_Tests}")]

<PageTitle>@GlobalValues.Component_Name_GoTo @GlobalValues.Common_Page_Title_Overview_Tests</PageTitle>

<section class="@GlobalValues.Info_Box_Class @GlobalValues.Info_Box_Coloured_Modifier">
    <h1 class="@GlobalValues.Info_Box_Heading_Class">@GlobalValues.Component_Name_GoTo @GlobalValues.Common_Page_Title_Overview_Tests</h1>

    <GoTo LabelText="GoTo overview and tests page heading" />

    <p>
        The GoTo component gives keyboard users a way to jump straight to the parts of a page that matter to them. Hidden <span class="@CodeSpan.Base">&lt;GoTo /&gt;</span> markers are placed on a page,
        with a short label shich can then can become visible active element from which a keyboard-only user can tab from. Leaving or closing the marker/label completely hides it again from
        users and assistive technologies.
    </p>
    <p>
        Markers that can't currently be reached, such as those inside a collapsed panel or hidden element are not included in the GoTo Viewers list.
    </p>
    <p>
        <b>Released: </b>Current version <b>v1.0.0</b> See the documentation site for more information: <a target="_blank" class="@GlobalValues.Link_Class" href="@GlobalValues.Site_Link_Docs_GoTo">GoTo documentation (opens in new window)</a>
    </p>
</section>

<section class="@GlobalValues.Info_Box_Class">

    <h2 class="@GlobalValues.Info_Box_Heading_Class">Information & instructions</h2>

    <p>
        <strong>Important</strong> Unlike the other components GoTo has not been tested with VoiceOver on the iPhone and as such is not supported.
    </p>
    <p>
        Open the GoTo Viewer using the shortcut key <span class="@Kbd.Base @Kbd.Scheme(KbdScheme.Neutral)">Alt + Z (Option + Z on macOS)</span>  or alternatively tab until the triggering button becomes visible
    </p>
    <p>
        Select and activate a button to set focus on the marker/label from where you can then tab from.
    </p>
    <h2 id="goto-keyboard" class="@GlobalValues.Info_Box_Heading_Class">Keyboard interaction</h2>
    <ul aria-labelledby="goto-keyboard">
        <li>
            <kbd class="@Kbd.Base @Kbd.Scheme(KbdScheme.Neutral)">Alt/Option + Z</kbd> - opens the viewer from anywhere on the page and places focus on it. It has no effect while a modal dialog is open.
        </li>
        <li>
            <kbd class="@Kbd.Base @Kbd.Scheme(KbdScheme.Neutral)">Tab</kbd> - places focus on the trigger button, which becomes visible while it has focus. Inside the viewer, it moves through the list buttons and then the close button.
            Tabbing out of the viewer closes it, and focus continues to the next element after the one that had focus before the viewer opened.
        </li>
        <li><kbd class="@Kbd.Base @Kbd.Scheme(KbdScheme.Neutral)">Shift + Tab</kbd> reverses tab direction, including leaving the viewer backwards.</li>
        <li>
            <kbd class="@Kbd.Base @Kbd.Scheme(KbdScheme.Neutral)">Space or Enter</kbd> - on the trigger button, opens the viewer. On a list button, closes the viewer, moves to the marker and shows its label.
        </li>
        <li>
            <kbd class="@Kbd.Base @Kbd.Scheme(KbdScheme.Neutral)">Down Arrow</kbd> - <kbd class="@Kbd.Base @Kbd.Scheme(KbdScheme.Neutral)">Up Arrow</kbd> - moves to the next or previous list button, stopping at the first and last.
        </li>
        <li>
            <kbd class="@Kbd.Base @Kbd.Scheme(KbdScheme.Neutral)">Home</kbd> - <kbd class="@Kbd.Base @Kbd.Scheme(KbdScheme.Neutral)">End</kbd> - moves to the first or last list button.
        </li>
        <li>
            <kbd class="@Kbd.Base @Kbd.Scheme(KbdScheme.Neutral)">Escape</kbd> - closes the viewer and returns focus to where it was before the viewer opened. When a marker's label is showing, it closes the label.
        </li>
    </ul>

</section>

<section class="@GlobalValues.Info_Box_Class">

    <h3 class="@GlobalValues.Info_Box_Heading_Class">Expectations</h3>

    <ul>
        <li>
            Selecting a button in the GoTo Viewer list should scroll its associated marker into view (on desktop devices) and highlight it with a pseudo focus ring.   
        </li>
        <li>
           Activating a button should set focus on the markers, now visible label which becomes the active element from where you can tab from.
        </li>
        <li>
            When the GoTo Viewer is open, pressing the <span class="@Kbd.Base @Kbd.Scheme(KbdScheme.Neutral)">Escape</span> key, tabbing away from or clicking outside of
            the viewer closes it, as does opening another popover such as the Announcement History viewer.
        </li>
        <li>
            When the GoTo label is open, pressing the <span class="@Kbd.Base @Kbd.Scheme(KbdScheme.Neutral)">Escape</span> key, tabbing away from or clicking outside of
            the labels region closes it, as does opening another popover.
        </li>
        <li>
            Voice control users should be able open, activate and navigat by using the visible accessible name with the appropriate commands, enter, tab, press, escape etc. 
            You can activate the hidden button (instead of tabbing to find) by saying all or part of its accessible name: "Go to viewer (Alt/Option+Z)"
        </li>
        <li>
            Once a marker/label is removed it should not be discoverable, focusable or cause a tab stop.
        </li>
    </ul>
</section>

<section class="section-box">
    <h3>GoTo test</h3>
    <GoTo LabelText="GoTo marker test page, before the tab stops" />
    <p>
        Open the viewer and either jump over the list of tab stops or go to any other available marker.
    </p>
    <ul>
        <li tabindex="0">Tab stop one.</li>
        <li tabindex="0">Tab stop two.</li>
        <li tabindex="0">Tab stop three.</li>
        <li tabindex="0">Tab stop four.</li>
        <li tabindex="0">Tab stop five.</li>
        <li tabindex="0">Tab stop six.</li>
        <li tabindex="0">Tab stop seven.</li>
        <li tabindex="0">Tab stop eight.</li>
        <li tabindex="0">Tab stop nine.</li>
        <li tabindex="0">Tab stop ten.</li>
    </ul>

</section>

